> import Prelude hiding (pred)

Currently, the interpreter uses an outermost reduction strategy. We can adapt the `reduce` function to implement an innermost reduction strategy (see InterpretInnermost.hs).

It is important that a substitition only happens on a given term, if there are not substitutions possible within that term or on a different more nested term. Anything else remains unchanged, that is, we make one step at a time and recude the left part of an application first.

Now we can use this module for the task at hand.

> import Exercises.Exercise4.Interpreter
> import Exercises.Exercise4.InterpreterInnermost

We're reusing the functionality of the given interpreter to define `fac`.

> fac = f :->: x :->: (cond :@: (isNull :@: Var x) :@: nat 1 :@: (mul :@: Var x :@: (Var f :@: (pred :@: Var x))))
>
> isNull = 'n' :->: (Var 'n' :@: (x :->: false)) :@: true
>
> mul = 'm' :->: 'n' :->: 'a' :->: Var 'm' :@: (Var 'n' :@: Var 'a')
>
> pred = 'n' :->: 'f' :->: 'x' :->: Var 'n' :@: ('g' :->: 'h' :->: Var 'h' :@: (Var 'g' :@: Var 'f')) :@: ('u' :->: Var 'x') :@: ('u' :->: Var 'u')

Let's start by using the outermost strategy.

    *OutermostInnermost> eval $ fix :@: fac :@: nat 1
    \a.\x.a x

